(a) It shall be the duty of the city manager to issue or refuse to issue or renew a license under this chapter to any applicant. In determining whether or not any application shall be granted and a license issued or renewed, the city manager shall consider the applicant or business's compliance with all other requirements of this chapter and the following information in the public interest and welfare:
(1) If the applicant and/or any holder of an interest in the licensed business has ever been convicted of or taken a plea of guilty or nolo contendere to any felony within ten years immediately prior to the filing of the application or any misdemeanor crime of moral turpitude within the five years immediately prior to the filing of the application;
(2) If the applicant, any holder of an interest in the licensed business, or any employee of the licensed business has been convicted of or taken a plea of guilty or nolo contendere to any law, regulation or ordinance involving alcoholic beverages, gambling, narcotics, or tax laws;
(3) If the applicant and/or any holder of an interest in the licensed business has ever violated any federal, state, county or municipal law, ordinance or administrative regulation related to the use, sale, manufacture, distribution, taxability, handling, possession, dealing or possession of beer, wine, or distilled spirits;
(4) The manner in which the applicant and/or any holder of an interest in the licensed business has conducted any business within the city, including any action or inactions of such persons which required an unusual or increased commitment of police resources or intervention, or fire department intervention, as compared to other licensees similarly situated. In considering this factor, the city manager may reference police incident reports, calls for service for police and fire, fire marshal reports indicating occupancy issues, or other documentation of resources utilized for maintaining good order and security in the area of the licensed premises;
(5) The reasonably anticipated effect on the immediately surrounding neighborhood for which the license is sought as to traffic congestion, noise and/or light pollution;
(6) Whether the applicant and/or any holder of an interest in the licensed business has ever had any alcoholic beverage or business license suspended or revoked by any state or any political subdivision thereof, or whether any alcoholic beverage business with which the applicant and/or any holder of an interest in the license has been associated has been cited for a violation of the laws or regulations of any state or any local ordinance pertaining to the use, sale, manufacture, distribution, taxability, handling, possession, dealing or possession of beer, wine, or distilled spirits alcoholic beverages, and the outcome of such proceedings;
(7) If the licensee, in the case of a renewal, has been chronically delinquent or in default in monthly excise tax reporting and remittance; "chronically" is deemed to be five months of the same 12-month calendar year;
(8) If it appears that the applicant would not have adequate financial participation in the proposed business to direct and manage its affairs, or where it appears that the application is intended to be a mere surrogate for a person or persons who would not otherwise qualify for a license for any reason whatsoever;
(b) If, upon investigation of an application for a license or renewal of an existing license, the city manager determines the applicant, any holder of an interest in the licensed business, or any of its employees have been convicted of or entered a plea of guilty or nolo contendre to any crime sufficient to warrant denial, or determines that granting the license is otherwise not within the public interest, the city manager shall have the option, in his or her discretion, to issue a probationary license conditioned upon completion of an action plan or satisfaction of conditions intended to reach compliance with the requirements of this chapter and/or mitigate the risks to the public interest or welfare. The action plan or conditions may include requiring the posting of a bond with adequate security in addition to license or renewal fees, requiring an off-duty City of Griffin POST certified officer or Spalding County Sheriff's Department Deputy for security on the licensed premises, requiring security cameras, and other similar actions.
(Ord. No. 25-005, § 1, 5-27-2025)
